Rima stepped back and looked critically at his handiwork. He gruntedwith satisfaction, and smiied down at his son''Well?'The new hut stood foursquare in the corner of the garden. The plaitedbamboo walls curled tightly round a stout frame of beech poles, cut andstripped by Rima with the big bush knife he had brought up from thetrade-store when Joseph had run down to tell him the news.'It's marvellous, father,' said Joseph. 'She won't get out of that.'He bent down and stroked the shining plumage of a large bird that layat his feet, trussed up with creepers- The bonds were too tight to allowrhe muscular body any movement, but the bird crossly jerked its headand opened its pointed beak to hiss at Joseph's fingers. The face andneck were coloured bright blue and red, and the head was topped withan oval dome of bone. The feathers were long and limp, hanging insilky strands. Under the cr+eper bindings twitched stumps of flightlesswings..That,s a fine cassowary,' Rima remarked. 'Did she give you muchtrouble?'
'No: after all, there were eight of us. Aieee! She ran like the wind!'ic The village boys had caught the cassowary that moming, coming onher suddenly in the bush and chasing her down a long slope. Her stronglegs were capable of taking her clear of pursuers on the level or goinguphill. But once she was on a downward incline she could only runfasterandfaster'squawkingwithfuryasshelostherbalance'til]shei, tripped over and fell in a tumble of feathers and feet'.i-i,.n Joseph and another boy, ourrunning the others, had flungrhemselvesonropoftheflappingbirdandheldherdown,whiletheremainingyounghuntershadtomdownbushcreepersforrope.Thetrickrest part wal keeping the feet with their fearsome claws underneath\,ru rhe body while she was lished up like a parcel. The u,hooping andlaughingboyscarriedherbacktothevillage'herheaddanglingdownfrom a branch slotted between her feet. Joseph and the other boy hacidrawnplayingcardsforher,andJoseph'StenofheartshadjustbeatenDavid Mali's nine of diamonds'i,-= Joseph was overjoyed to have his own cassowary- She would alwaysbe bad_tempered, iiuur" to peck out an eye or lash out dangerously withrhose sharpclaws. But safely shut in her new horne, fed on taro scraPsandberries,shewouldgrowfat;andonedayinthefurureshewouldmalie a tough but tasty ioast for a clan gathering- Then the story of therc capture would be told, with Joseph as the hero'
